from gen import *
from data_rx import R
def A(slug,*a,**k):
    R(slug,*a,**k); D[slug]["cat"]="atypical"

R("lean","Lean (purple drank, sizzurp)","A mixture of prescription cough syrup containing codeine and promethazine (an antihistamine and anti-nausea drug), usually mixed with soda and hard candy.",
"Street names: lean, purple drank, sizzurp, drank, barre, Texas tea. Source syrups historically included Actavis Promethazine with Codeine (discontinued by Actavis in 2014), Tussionex, and Phenergan with Codeine.",
"Lean grew out of Houston's Third Ward and South Side in the 1960s to 1980s among blues musicians and then rap artists. DJ Screw (Robert Earl Davis Jr.) created 'chopped and screwed' music in the early 1990s, slowed down to mirror the slow, heavy feeling of the drink, and his Screwed Up Click spread the culture. Songs like Three 6 Mafia's 'Sippin' on Some Syrup' (2000) took it national. DJ Screw died in 2000, Big Moe in 2007, and Pimp C of UGK in 2007, with codeine and promethazine implicated; Lil Wayne and others were hospitalized with seizures. Actavis stopped making its syrup in 2014 partly because of its fame in music.",
"The source syrups are prescribed for cough and cold symptoms. Lean itself has no medical use.",
"Lean is used socially, often sipped over hours from double-stacked foam cups. Counterfeit 'lean' made from other syrups, pills, or fentanyl-laced mixtures is now common.",
"Codeine-promethazine syrup is US Schedule V. Possessing it without a prescription is illegal.",
["Lean is described as a slow, syrupy, heavy-bodied high, very different from codeine tablets alone. Promethazine adds deep sedation, a weighted, dreamy, slowed-down feeling, and a sense that time is dragging, which is the experience 'screwed' music was built to imitate. People describe feeling relaxed, 'leaned back', sleepy, and detached, with mild euphoria and itch from codeine.",
"Compared with plain codeine, lean feels sleepier, more sluggish, and more disorienting; speech slows, movements become clumsy, and many fall asleep wherever they are. Its sweet taste and sipping style mean people often keep drinking without noticing how sedated they are."],
["Sipped slowly, lean begins working in 20 to 45 minutes: a warm, heavy feeling in the limbs and a softening of the mind.",
"The come-up is gradual. Promethazine's drowsiness and codeine's warmth build together; people describe their body getting heavy and their movements slowing down, with an itch on the face or chest.",
"The peak, around 1 to 2 hours, is a dreamy, deeply relaxed, almost underwater feeling. Speech and reactions slow, eyelids droop, and music and time feel stretched. Breathing slows, and with alcohol or pills this is when it becomes dangerous.",
"The plateau can last several hours with continued sipping, with nodding, napping, and a 'stuck on the couch' feeling. Promethazine can cause restlessness in the legs, confusion, and in some people strange dreams or agitation.",
"The comedown is drowsy and foggy; many simply sleep for many hours.",
"After-effects include a heavy hangover, dry mouth, constipation, and grogginess. Promethazine can cause a lingering, restless, 'can't get comfortable' feeling.",
"The next day is often sluggish. Regular lean use leads to codeine dependence, weight gain and tooth decay from the sugar, and withdrawal with insomnia, sweating, and irritability."],
{"oral":"Drunk, mixed with soda."},"Oral only.",
"Promethazine and codeine both depress breathing and add to each other. Adding alcohol, benzodiazepines, or Xanax bars is a common and deadly pattern. Promethazine can also cause seizures, dangerous heart rhythms, and, in children under 2, fatal breathing suppression. Counterfeit lean may contain fentanyl. People can stop breathing in their sleep.",
["Agnich LE, et al. Purple drank prevalence and characteristics (Am J Drug Alcohol Abuse, 2013)","Peters RJ, et al. Codeine cough syrup misuse (Am J Health Behav, 2007)","FDA label, promethazine with codeine","Hart M. 'DJ Screw' (Texas Monthly)"],
times={"oral":["20-45 min","45-90 min","1-2 h","2-6 h","4-8 h","8-24 h","next day"]})
D["lean"]["cat"]="opioid"

A("tianeptine","Tianeptine ('gas station heroin')","Atypical antidepressant (tricyclic structure) that is also a full mu-opioid agonist, discovered in 2014.",
"Prescription: Stablon, Coaxil, Tatinol (Europe, Asia, Latin America). US products: Zaza, Tianaa, Pegasus, Neptune's Fix, sold in gas stations and smoke shops as 'supplements'.",
"Developed by the French company Servier in the 1960s and marketed as an antidepressant in 1989. In 2014, researchers at Columbia showed it activates mu-opioid receptors (Gassaway et al.). It was never approved in the US, but from the 2010s it was sold in convenience stores. The FDA warned against it in 2018, 2022, and again in 2025 after clusters of seizures, comas, and deaths, some linked to Neptune's Fix products that also contained synthetic cannabinoids. Several US states have banned it.",
"Prescribed for depression and anxiety in some countries. Not approved in the US.",
"Sold as a 'nootropic' or mood supplement, it became known as 'gas station heroin' because people develop opioid-like dependence and sometimes use it many times a day.",
"Not federally controlled in the US, but banned in a growing number of states (including Michigan, Alabama, Florida, Ohio, and others). Prescription-only elsewhere; banned in the UK under the Psychoactive Substances Act.",
["Tianeptine's effect is described as a short, clean, mildly stimulating opioid-like euphoria: mood lift, motivation, and calm, with a light body warmth. At higher amounts it becomes more clearly opioid-like, with itch, nausea, and sedation.",
"Because it lasts only 2 to 3 hours, many people take it repeatedly through the day. Users describe the withdrawal as disproportionately harsh compared with how mild the high felt."],
["Swallowed, it starts in about 30 to 60 minutes with a subtle mood lift.","The come-up is quick, 15 to 30 minutes: calm, a bit of energy, and a warm glow.","The peak, about 60 to 90 minutes in, is a mild euphoria with motivation and lowered anxiety; at higher amounts, opioid warmth, itch, and nodding.","Plateau is short.","Comedown begins around 2 hours: a fairly abrupt drop in mood, sometimes with irritability.","After-effects can include nausea, headache, and restlessness.","With daily repeated use, withdrawal sets in quickly: severe anxiety, sweating, diarrhea, agitation, and insomnia, sometimes lasting weeks."],
{"oral":"Tablets (prescription), capsules, powders, and liquid shots sold in stores."},"Injection of tianeptine powder has been reported and caused serious harm, but is not covered.",
"Seizures, comas, and dangerous heart effects have been reported, especially from US store products, some of which were contaminated. Naloxone reverses its opioid effects. Overdose is much more likely when combined with other sedatives.",
["PsychonautWiki, Tianeptine (onset 30-60 min, total 2-3 h)","Gassaway MM, et al. Tianeptine is a mu-opioid receptor agonist (Transl Psychiatry, 2014)","FDA warnings about tianeptine (2018, 2022, 2025)","CDC MMWR, tianeptine exposures reported to poison centers (2018)"],
times={"oral":["30-60 min","15-30 min","60-90 min","under 1 h","2-3 h","3-6 h","next day"]})

A("kratom","Kratom (mitragynine)","Leaves of Mitragyna speciosa, a tree in the coffee family from Southeast Asia. The main alkaloid, mitragynine, is a partial mu-opioid agonist; a small amount converts into the stronger 7-hydroxymitragynine in the body.",
"Sold as powder, capsules, extracts, and shots. Strains marketed by vein color (red, green, white) and origin (Maeng Da, Bali, Borneo).",
"Kratom leaves have been chewed by laborers in Thailand and Malaysia for centuries to fight fatigue and ease pain. Thailand banned it in 1943 and legalized it again in 2021. It spread to the US around the 2000s, sold as a supplement. In 2016 the DEA announced plans to make it Schedule I but withdrew after public protest.",
"No approved medical use. Many people use it on their own to manage pain or opioid withdrawal.",
"Used for energy, mood, pain, and self-managing opioid withdrawal or dependence.",
"Not federally controlled in the US; banned in several states (Alabama, Arkansas, Indiana, Rhode Island, Vermont, Wisconsin) and regulated in others. Controlled in many countries.",
["Kratom is unusual: at lower levels it acts like a stimulant (energy, talkativeness, focus), and at higher levels more like a mild opioid (calm, warmth, pain relief, sedation). People describe its euphoria as subtle, a grounded contentment rather than a rush.",
"Unpleasant effects include nausea, a 'wobbly' feeling of dizzy eyes and vomiting if too much is taken, constipation, and a bitter taste. Compared with prescription opioids, it is weaker and less euphoric, but daily use does cause dependence."],
["Swallowed, effects begin in 10 to 30 minutes, often as a mild lift in energy or calm.","The come-up brings increased sociability or relaxation depending on the amount, with a warm feeling in the stomach.","The peak, around 1 to 2 hours, is described as content, comfortable, and pain-free, sometimes stimulated, sometimes drowsy. Pupils may narrow.","The plateau is steady and functional for a couple of hours.","Effects fade over 3 to 6 hours.","After-effects include constipation, dry mouth, and sometimes a headache or low energy.","Daily users describe withdrawal similar to mild opioid withdrawal: irritability, runny nose, muscle aches, jerky limbs, and insomnia, lasting several days."],
{"oral":"Powdered leaf, capsules, teas, extracts, and liquid shots."},"Other routes are not typical.",
"Overdose from kratom alone is rare but has occurred, especially with concentrated extracts. Most deaths involve other drugs. Liver injury, seizures, and adulteration (including with opioids or 7-OH) have been reported.",
["PsychonautWiki, Kratom","NIDA, Kratom research report","FDA statements on kratom","Kruegel AC, Grundmann O. The medicinal chemistry and neuropharmacology of kratom (Neuropharmacology, 2018)"],
times={"oral":["10-30 min","30-60 min","1-2 h","2-3 h","3-6 h","6-12 h","next day"]})

A("7-hydroxymitragynine","7-OH (7-hydroxymitragynine)","A kratom alkaloid and metabolite of mitragynine; a much stronger mu-opioid agonist than mitragynine. Sold as concentrated, semi-synthetic products.",
"Sold as tablets and shots under names like 7-OH, 7OHMZ, Pseudo, and many brand-named products in smoke shops.",
"Found in tiny amounts in kratom leaf, 7-OH was identified as a strong opioid in 2002. From around 2023 to 2024, highly concentrated 7-OH products appeared in US smoke shops. In July 2025 the FDA recommended scheduling 7-OH under federal law, warning that it is far stronger than kratom.",
"No approved medical use.",
"Marketed as a 'kratom' product but used like an opioid, especially by people with past opioid use.",
"FDA recommended Schedule I control in 2025; several states have banned 7-OH products. Check current law.",
["7-OH is described as a much more typical opioid high than kratom: warm, euphoric, sedating, and itchy, closer to oxycodone than to leaf kratom. Users describe it as smooth and potent.","Because it is often marketed alongside kratom, people expect something mild and are surprised by its strength and how quickly dependence develops."],
["Swallowed, effects begin within 15 to 30 minutes with warmth and calm.","A smooth build of euphoria and body heaviness.","Peak around 1 hour: opioid-like warmth, contentment, itch, and nodding.","Plateau of a couple of hours.","Fades over 3 to 5 hours.","Constipation and tiredness.","Daily users describe opioid-like withdrawal, often stronger than kratom withdrawal."],
{"oral":"Tablets, gummies, and liquid shots."},"Other routes not covered.",
"Breathing suppression like other opioids, especially with alcohol or sedatives. Product strengths vary. Naloxone reverses it.",
["FDA, 7-OH scheduling recommendation and press release (July 2025)","Matsumoto K, et al. 7-hydroxymitragynine (Life Sci, 2004)"])

A("mgm-15","MGM-15 and mitragynine pseudoindoxyl","Semi-synthetic derivatives related to kratom alkaloids. Mitragynine pseudoindoxyl is a rearrangement product of 7-OH; MGM-15 is a name used for a research-derived kratom analog sold in some online and smoke-shop products.",
"MGM-15 and 'pseudo' products; often mislabeled.",
"Mitragynine pseudoindoxyl was described in research in the 2010s (Váradi et al., 2016) as a potent mu agonist with less breathing suppression in mice. MGM-15 and similar compounds appeared on the US gray market in 2024 to 2025.",
"None.","Marketed to people who use kratom or 7-OH products.","Uncontrolled federally in most cases but covered by some state bans; legal status is changing quickly.",
["There is almost no reliable information about what these compounds feel like in humans. Reports describe opioid-like warmth, sedation, and euphoria, sometimes said to be very strong.","Animal research claims of 'safer' breathing effects have not been tested in people, and products are unregulated and often mislabeled."],
["Effects reportedly begin within 15 to 45 minutes when swallowed.","Opioid-like warmth builds.","Peak reported as strong opioid-like euphoria.","Duration unknown, reported as several hours.","Fades gradually.","Unknown.","Dependence and withdrawal are likely."],
{"oral":"Tablets and liquids."},"Other routes not covered.",
"Unknown potency and purity. Treat as a strong opioid; naloxone is expected to reverse it.",
["Váradi A, et al. Mitragynine pseudoindoxyl (J Med Chem, 2016)","FDA 2025 statements on concentrated kratom alkaloid products"])

A("akuamma","Akuamma (akuammine)","Seeds of Picralima nitida, a West African tree, containing akuammine and related alkaloids that weakly activate opioid receptors.",
"Sold as powdered seeds and capsules.",
"Used in traditional West African medicine for pain, fever, and malaria. Sold online in the West since the 2010s.",
"No approved medical use.","Used occasionally as a kratom alternative.","Not controlled in the US.",
["Akuamma is described as a mild, sometimes barely noticeable calm with slight pain relief and relaxation, much weaker than kratom. Many report a bitter taste and nausea.","Research suggests its alkaloids are weak opioid agonists, so the experience is subtle and variable."],
["Begins 30 to 60 minutes after swallowing.","Mild relaxation.","Subtle calm.","A few hours of mild effect.","Fades.","Possible nausea.","Little known about dependence."],
{"oral":"Seed powder and capsules."},"Other routes not covered.","Little studied; combine risk with other sedatives.",
["Creed SM, et al. Akuamma alkaloids at opioid receptors (J Nat Prod, 2021)"])

A("loperamide","Loperamide (misuse)","Synthetic opioid used for diarrhea. At medical amounts it barely enters the brain because a pump (P-glycoprotein) pushes it out.",
"Imodium, Imodium A-D, generics.",
"Developed by Janssen in 1969 and sold over the counter in the US since 1988. From about 2015, reports grew of people misusing very large amounts to self-treat opioid withdrawal or for an effect, leading to heart deaths. The FDA added warnings in 2016 and limited package sizes in 2018.",
"Diarrhea.","Called the 'poor man's methadone' for self-treating withdrawal.","Over the counter, not controlled.",
["At medical amounts, loperamide has no psychoactive effect. Misuse produces weak, inconsistent opioid effects at best, and many people feel mostly constipation and heart palpitations.","The overwhelming danger is to the heart, not the high."],
["Effects (if any) begin slowly over hours.","Mostly gut effects.","Minimal felt peak.","Long-lasting constipation.","Very long duration.","Constipation can be severe.","Heart effects may persist."],
{"oral":"Tablets, capsules, and liquid."},"Oral only.",
"Misuse at high amounts causes dangerous heart rhythms (QT prolongation, torsades de pointes), fainting, and cardiac arrest. Naloxone may not fix heart rhythm problems. Seek emergency care for palpitations or fainting.",
["FDA Drug Safety Communications on loperamide (2016, 2018)","Eggleston W, et al. Loperamide abuse associated with cardiac dysrhythmia (Ann Emerg Med, 2017)"])

A("salvinorin-kappa","Salvinorin A and kappa-opioid agonists","Salvinorin A, from Salvia divinorum, is a potent kappa-opioid agonist, not a mu agonist. Included because it acts on opioid receptors, but its effects are hallucinogenic, not euphoric.",
"Salvia, diviner's sage, 'Sally D'. Related research kappa agonists include U-50488 and enadoline.",
"Mazatec healers in Oaxaca, Mexico, have used Salvia divinorum in ceremonies for centuries. Its active compound was identified in 1982, and in 2002 Bryan Roth's lab showed it is a kappa-opioid agonist. YouTube videos of people smoking salvia led many US states to ban it around 2008 to 2012.",
"None approved; studied in research.","Smoked or chewed for intense, short hallucinations.","Not federally controlled in the US; banned in many states. UK Psychoactive Substances Act.",
["Salvia is nothing like other opioids. People describe sudden, overwhelming dissociation: feeling pulled, merged with objects, reality folding or 'zippering', loss of body, and often fear or dysphoria. Euphoria is rare.","Kappa agonists tend to cause dysphoria, which is why they have been studied as non-addictive painkillers and are not used for pleasure."],
["Smoked, effects begin within 15 to 60 seconds.","Reality shifts rapidly.","Peak at 1 to 5 minutes: intense dissociation and hallucinations.","Lasts a few minutes.","Fades over 10 to 20 minutes.","Confusion and an afterglow or unease.","Usually normal next day."],
{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked leaf or extract.","sublingual-buccal":"Leaves chewed and held in the mouth, the traditional route."},"Other routes not covered.",
"Overdose like mu opioids does not happen, but people can injure themselves during the experience. Naloxone does not reliably help. Have a sober sitter.",
["Roth BL, et al. Salvinorin A is a kappa opioid agonist (PNAS, 2002)","PsychonautWiki, Salvinorin A","Erowid Salvia divinorum vault"],
times={"inhaled-smoked":["15-60 sec","under 1 min","1-5 min","5-10 min","10-20 min","20-60 min","next day"],"sublingual-buccal":["5-15 min","10-20 min","20-40 min","30-60 min","1-1.5 h","1-2 h","next day"]})
